WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:06.010
So after Bernie's win in New Hampshire, you
had coming out of the woodwork endless.

00:00:06.010 --> 00:00:11.490
Supposedly enlightened takes on the meaning
of this victory for Bernie, the meaning of

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:18.340
this loss for Biden, the of this surge by
Amy Klobuchar, and I don't want to be rude

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:25.140
or disrespectful to anybody, but the meaning
of this is actually quite limited and most

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:29.750
of these enlightened hot takes are not particularly
enlightened.

00:00:29.750 --> 00:00:34.899
Bernie won a primary by 1.5 percentage points
tied in the number of delegates with Pete

00:00:34.899 --> 00:00:40.610
Buddha, judge nine each, and now has two popular
vote wins in the two primaries that we've

00:00:40.610 --> 00:00:41.949
had so far.

00:00:41.949 --> 00:00:43.620
The future is wide open.

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:44.620
Okay.

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:49.649
The circumstances of this particular primary,
in addition to this, just being the first

00:00:49.649 --> 00:00:54.430
to tell us very little about who is ultimately
going to be the nominees.

00:00:54.430 --> 00:00:56.359
So let's go through some of the talking points.

00:00:56.359 --> 00:00:58.539
There are pro and anti Bernie talking points
in there.

00:00:58.539 --> 00:01:00.650
There's a whole bunch of different stuff.

00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:01.760
Let's talk through them.

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:04.199
Some of the takes are just straight up dumb.

00:01:04.199 --> 00:01:05.199
Okay.

00:01:05.199 --> 00:01:09.282
One was the results from New Hampshire are
really bad for Bernie because in 2016, he

00:01:09.282 --> 00:01:14.049
got 60% of the primary vote and this time
he got 25% of the primary vote.

00:01:14.049 --> 00:01:15.049
Come on.

00:01:15.049 --> 00:01:16.049
Okay.

00:01:16.049 --> 00:01:17.049
There were two real candidates in 2016.

00:01:17.049 --> 00:01:18.049
Yeah.

00:01:18.049 --> 00:01:22.310
Uh, I think, um, uh, you know, you had um,
uh, what, what was it, the dude's name?

00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:25.990
Uh, vermin Supreme got 300 votes or something.

00:01:25.990 --> 00:01:30.670
In New Hampshire in 2016, there were really
two candidates this time.

00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:35.740
You have five people coming away with delegates
in New Hampshire to say Bernie is doing poorly

00:01:35.740 --> 00:01:37.790
because he got 26% instead of 60.

00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:40.009
Last time is a meaningless take.

00:01:40.009 --> 00:01:42.119
It tells us nothing whatsoever.

00:01:42.119 --> 00:01:46.990
A second talking point, Bernie underperformed
the polling.

00:01:46.990 --> 00:01:48.940
Bernie was a Bernie underperformed.

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:53.420
His results underperformed polling by about
three percentage points.

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:56.750
Thus, clearly Bernie is on the decline.

00:01:56.750 --> 00:02:03.299
Bernie underperformed by 2.8 he still won
the delegate count for him and Pete is identical

00:02:03.299 --> 00:02:09.170
nine each from New Hampshire and almost every
delegate is still up for grabs.

00:02:09.170 --> 00:02:13.100
There's a candidate in Michael Bloomberg who
hasn't even been on the ballot yet, who is

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:17.800
pulling 15% nationally now, which we will
talk about a little bit later.

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:23.420
The Bernie underperformance of two points
to 2.8 points is meaningless in the big picture.

00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:25.680
Don't fall for that anti Bernie take.

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:30.930
Now you also have some pro Bernie talking
points that are not particularly meaningful.

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:36.410
I heard one floating around yesterday that
no candidate has lost when they win Iowa and

00:02:36.410 --> 00:02:38.260
New Hampshire in recent history.

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:42.680
Now that may or may not be true depending
on how far back you go, but it's even a weird

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:48.200
technicality because Bernie did not get the
most delegates in Iowa, so by the way that

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:50.620
it is measured, Bernie didn't win Iowa.

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:52.420
Bernie did get the most votes.

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:56.090
Either way, it's a fight about something that's
not relevant.

00:02:56.090 --> 00:02:59.530
It just does not actually tell us what's going
to happen in this primary.

00:02:59.530 --> 00:03:06.920
Another talking point floating around is that
Bernie or Pete or whoever, that they can't

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:13.280
necessarily coalesce the nonwhite vote and
thus the results in the early white States

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:14.280
don't matter.

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:18.870
Well, if you look at the polling for Bernie,
it's very different than Pete with black voters

00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:20.180
and Latino voters.

00:03:20.180 --> 00:03:21.250
That's number one.

00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:25.740
Just because there haven't been large Latino
and black populations that have voted yet,

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:27.500
doesn't mean that you can make that claim.

00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:29.040
We do have polling about that.

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:36.560
It is true that the results in the early white
States reflect the demographics of those States,

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:37.900
but they're still important.

00:03:37.900 --> 00:03:41.560
They dictate the publicity and the headlines
around the primary.

00:03:41.560 --> 00:03:47.840
They can provide a PR lift and case in point,
we've seen that the support for candidates

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:53.890
from the black and Latino communities has
shifted as candidates have done better and

00:03:53.890 --> 00:03:56.700
worse nationally and in the early primary.

00:03:56.700 --> 00:03:59.310
So that's not a particularly good talking
point either.

00:03:59.310 --> 00:04:02.710
Here's the reality and a lot of you are going
to be bored by the reality or you'll find

00:04:02.710 --> 00:04:06.510
it sort of uninteresting because we want to
feel like everything's already happened and

00:04:06.510 --> 00:04:09.180
really very little has the numbers.

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:13.330
So far, tell us some things and they don't
tell us other things.

00:04:13.330 --> 00:04:19.490
The numbers so far suggest we will have four
to five candidates staying in this mathematically,

00:04:19.490 --> 00:04:22.190
at least hypothetically viable for a while.

00:04:22.190 --> 00:04:25.400
We should expect that five candidates already
have delegates.

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:29.250
I expect Tom Stier will get delegates in South
Carolina.

00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:32.410
Uh, that will be a sixth candidate with delegates.

00:04:32.410 --> 00:04:36.150
I expect Michael Bloomberg will get delegates
on super Tuesday.

00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:38.890
He's pulling in the teens in Texas and Florida.

00:04:38.890 --> 00:04:40.130
We'll talk about that later.

00:04:40.130 --> 00:04:42.640
That's a bunch of delegates for Bloomberg.

00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:47.860
So one thing so far is that this will likely
be a big field for a while.

00:04:47.860 --> 00:04:54.020
Number two, the dynamics of this race could
change very quickly depending on what happens

00:04:54.020 --> 00:04:55.100
with Michael Bloomberg.

00:04:55.100 --> 00:04:58.260
His polling has exploded, which I want to
get to.

00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:03.370
He had this horrible racist audio released
by Benjamin Dixon, our friend two days ago.

00:05:03.370 --> 00:05:08.020
His campaign is trying to do crisis management
around the racist audio.

00:05:08.020 --> 00:05:13.490
We will see how his polling is affected by
that, but right now, every indication that

00:05:13.490 --> 00:05:14.970
Bloomberg will be a factor.

00:05:14.970 --> 00:05:19.880
I still believe Bloomberg ends up third or
fourth in terms of total delegates.

00:05:19.880 --> 00:05:26.260
By the time this is done, if Biden keeps failing
and Bloomberg comes in strong on super Tuesday,

00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:30.450
the DNC establishment will have to make a
decision which will have a big impact on the

00:05:30.450 --> 00:05:34.320
rest of the race and until they do, we won't
know what's going to happen.

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:41.330
Will the establishment abandoned Biden and
get behind Pete or Amy or will the establishment

00:05:41.330 --> 00:05:46.420
abandoned Biden and get behind a Bloomberg
if Biden's failures continue?

00:05:46.420 --> 00:05:47.420
These are real questions.

00:05:47.420 --> 00:05:52.520
You know, whether the, whether a popular vote
win in Iowa and New Hampshire guarantees a

00:05:52.520 --> 00:05:56.590
primary victory is not a particularly relevant
or interesting question.

00:05:56.590 --> 00:05:59.940
This is a very different primary than one's
past.

00:05:59.940 --> 00:06:03.210
Everyone wants to know now what the path is.

00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:07.080
It's not knowable right now because of the
unique nature of this race, including that

00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:12.270
we have a candidate who's not yet been on
any ballot, but it's polling as high as 17%

00:06:12.270 --> 00:06:14.020
in a recent national poll.

00:06:14.020 --> 00:06:15.020
That's Michael Bloomberg.

00:06:15.020 --> 00:06:17.230
We'll look at those numbers a little bit later.

00:06:17.230 --> 00:06:18.350
So far.

00:06:18.350 --> 00:06:19.350
Let me know what you think.

